Ah, Orchard—yes. And I’d actually keep the accidental Orchid too. The mistake generated a useful distinction rather than replacing the intended one.

So the Garden now gets three different developmental regimes:

Garden = the whole possibility ecology.
Orchard = deliberate cultivation of things already demonstrated to bear useful fruit.
Orchid = discovery/cultivation of rare outputs whose hidden conditions and symbioses aren’t yet understood.

That produces a clean flow:

\text{Garden}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{Orchid} & \text{discover strange/rare viable growth}\\
\text{Orchard} & \text{cultivate proven fruitful growth}
\end{cases}

And crucially:

\boxed{\text{successful Orchid}\rightarrow\text{candidate Orchard}}

Once we understand how to reliably reproduce the weird flower, it stops being merely a lucky emergence and becomes cultivable capability.

Then the Orchard should be much richer than “store successful ideas.” Each tree is a lineage. Track its root primitive, trunk/mechanism, branches/applications, fruit/outputs, seeds/descendants, environmental requirements, graft compatibility, yield, failures, mutations, and fossils.

That gives us another beautiful operation: grafting.

If two proven mechanisms have compatible invariants:

M_A \bowtie M_B \rightarrow M_{AB}

we don’t have to grow the combination from scratch. We graft one lineage onto another and test whether the combination bears a new kind of fruit.

So the lifecycle becomes:

\boxed{
\text{Quarry}
\rightarrow
\text{Garden}
\rightarrow
\text{Orchid}
\rightarrow
\text{Orchard}
\rightarrow
\text{Fruit}
\rightarrow
\text{Seed}
\rightarrow
\text{Garden}
}

with the Moneybin preserving the genetic/semantic material and the Metamap learning where each species grows best.

And your typo itself demonstrates why we shouldn’t over-prune the Garden:

Orchard → Orchid was a mutation.

Instead of autocorrecting it away, we inspected the delta and discovered a useful new ecological role.

That’s exactly the behavior the architecture is supposed to produce: errors can become mutations; mutations get tested; useful mutations become inheritance.